Java does not use `key.hashCode()` directly because raw hash codes often have poor bit distribution — especially in the lower bits that determine the bucket index when the table is small. To fix this, Java applies a **spread function** that mixes the upper and lower halves of the hash:

```java
static final int hash(Object key) {
    int h;
    // XOR the top 16 bits into the bottom 16 bits
    // This ensures both halves of the hash contribute to the bucket index
    return (key == null) ? 0 : (h = key.hashCode()) ^ (h >>> 16);
}
```

The resulting hash is then masked with the table size to get the bucket index:

```java
// capacity is always a power of 2 (16, 32, 64...)
// so (capacity - 1) is a bitmask: 15 = 0b1111, 31 = 0b11111, etc.
int index = (capacity - 1) & hash(key);
```

This bitwise AND is mathematically equivalent to `hash % capacity`, but faster because it avoids division. This is also why HashMap enforces power-of-two capacity — it makes this fast modulo trick possible.

Resizing is the **hidden performance cost** of HashMap. The default initial capacity is **16** and load factor is **0.75**, meaning the map triggers a resize when just 12 entries are added. During resize, Java allocates a new array of **double the size**, then iterates every existing entry and recalculates its bucket index — this is an O(n) operation that happens on the thread that triggered it.

In high-throughput services, this resize-and-rehash can cause visible **p99 latency spikes** because one unlucky request pays the cost for all previous insertions. The fix is straightforward — **pre-size the map** when you have a reasonable estimate of the number of entries:

```java
// Without pre-sizing: starts at 16, resizes at 12, 24, 48, 96...
// Each resize copies ALL existing entries to the new array
Map<String, Integer> bad = new HashMap<>();  // default capacity 16

// With pre-sizing: allocate enough upfront
// Formula: expectedEntries / loadFactor → next power of two
// For ~1000 entries: 1000 / 0.75 ≈ 1334 → next power of 2 = 2048
Map<String, Integer> good = new HashMap<>(2048);  // no resize until 1536 entries
```

During rehashing, Java 8 uses a clever optimization: since the new capacity is exactly double, each entry either stays at its current index or moves to `oldIndex + oldCapacity`. This means the rehash doesn't need to recompute hashes — it just checks one additional bit of the existing hash.

The **hashCode/equals contract** is the single most common source of HashMap bugs. The rule: if two objects are equal via `equals()`, they must return the same `hashCode()`. If this is violated, `put` stores the entry in one bucket but `get` looks in a different bucket — the entry is silently lost.

Here's a concrete demonstration of the bug and the fix:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class HashMapDemo {

    // BAD: overrides equals but NOT hashCode
    // Two BadKey("x") objects are equal, but their hashCodes differ
    // because hashCode() falls back to Object's identity-based default
    record BadKey(String value) {
        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
            return o instanceof BadKey b && Objects.equals(value, b.value);
        }
    }

    // GOOD: Java record auto-generates BOTH equals and hashCode
    // based on all declared fields — no manual override needed
    record GoodKey(String value) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Broken: put and get create separate BadKey instances
        // They are equals(), but have different hashCodes → different buckets
        var broken = new HashMap<BadKey, String>();
        broken.put(new BadKey("x"), "hello");
        System.out.println(broken.get(new BadKey("x"))); // null — LOST!

        // Fixed: GoodKey auto-generates consistent hashCode from fields
        var working = new HashMap<GoodKey, String>();
        working.put(new GoodKey("x"), "hello");
        System.out.println(working.get(new GoodKey("x"))); // "hello" ✓
    }
}
```

A useful **HashMap pattern** for interviews is the frequency count using `merge()` — it's cleaner than the `getOrDefault` + `put` pattern:

```java
public static Map<Character, Integer> frequency(String s) {
    Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        // merge: if key absent, insert value 1
        //        if key present, apply Integer::sum to old and new values
        freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
    }
    return freq;
}
// frequency("banana") → {b=1, a=3, n=2}
```
